NNAAMMEE
     cchhffllaaggss — change file flags

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     cchhffllaaggss [--hh] [--RR [--HH | --LL | --PP]] _f_l_a_g_s _f_i_l_e _._._.

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The cchhffllaaggss utility modifies the file flags of the listed files as speci‐
     fied by the _f_l_a_g_s operand.  The _f_l_a_g_s of a file dictate special restric‐
     tions beyond those enforced by its mode/permissions.  Only the superuser
     can change the user flags on block and character devices.

     You can use llss --lloo to see the flags of existing files.

     The options are as follows:

     --HH      If the --RR option is also specified, symbolic links on the command
             line are followed.  Symbolic links encountered in the tree tra‐
             versal are not followed.

     --hh      Treat symbolic links like other files: modify links instead of
             following them.  The --hh and --RR options are mutually exclusive.

     --LL      If the --RR option is also specified, all symbolic links are fol‐
             lowed.

     --PP      If the --RR option is also specified, no symbolic links are fol‐
             lowed.

     --RR      Recurse.  Where _f_i_l_e is a directory, change the flags of the di‐
             rectory and all the files and directories in the file hierarchy
             below it.

     Flags are a comma separated list of keywords.  The following keywords are
     currently defined:

           aarrcchh    set the archived flag (superuser only)
           nnoodduummpp  set the nodump flag (owner or superuser only)
           ssaappppnndd  set the system append-only flag (superuser only)
           sscchhgg    set the system immutable flag (superuser only)
           uuaappppnndd  set the user append-only flag (owner or superuser only)
           uucchhgg    set the user immutable flag (owner or superuser only)

     The aarrcchh flag is for compatibility only, and currently has no effect.

     A file with the nnoodduummpp flag set will by default only be backed up by
     dump(8) during full backups.  The --hh option of dump(8) can be used to al‐
     ter this.

     An immutable file may not be changed, moved, or deleted.  An append-only
     file is immutable except that data may be appended to it.

     The superuser-settable ssaappppnndd and sscchhgg flags can be set at any time, but
     may only be cleared when the system is running at security level 0 or -1
     (insecure or permanently insecure mode, respectively).  For more informa‐
     tion on setting the system security level, see securelevel(7).

     Putting the letters nnoo before a flag name causes the flag to be turned
     off.  For example:

           nnoouucchhgg  the immutable bit should be cleared

     The --HH, --LL, and --PP options are ignored unless the --RR option is specified.
     In addition, these options override each other and the command's actions
     are determined by the last one specified.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The cchhffllaaggss utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

